#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/math-academy-id 280 :topic/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "c2580f8e-3ba3-5aa9-b1f1-0d8e49986381" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/math-academy-id 1110 :topic/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/math-academy-id 2225 :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622 :topic/math-academy-id 356 :topic/title "Solving Two-Variable Equations"}] :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x - y = 4; x + 2y = 16$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4 + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x+2y &=16 \\\\ (4+y) + 2y &=16 \\\\ 4+3y &=16 \\\\ 3y &=12 \\\\ y &=4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into\n\n$$\nx=4+y\n$$\n\n, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= 4 + y \\\\ &= 4 + (4) \\\\ &=8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${2u + 9v - 6 = 0; 2u + 6v - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n{2u + 9v - 6 = 0(1); 2u + 6v - 8 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+6v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 3v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2+3v &= 0 \\\\ 3v &= -2 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-12 &= 0 \\\\ 2u &= 12 \\\\ u &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048216 :topic/math-academy-id 5135 :topic/title "Proving Perpendicular Line Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048646 :topic/math-academy-id 6305 :topic/title "Surface Areas of Combined Prisms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048873 :topic/math-academy-id 6280 :topic/title "Determining Unknown Parameters in Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048975 :topic/math-academy-id 6252 :topic/title "Linear Functions Containing Unknown Parameters"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048991 :topic/math-academy-id 5271 :topic/title "Further Proving Alternate Angle Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049198 :topic/math-academy-id 890 :topic/title "Modeling Using Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326 :topic/math-academy-id 2026 :topic/title "Moments and Center of Mass"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050365 :topic/math-academy-id 1354 :topic/title "Properties of Parallelograms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564 :topic/math-academy-id 1081 :topic/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Decimal Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050661 :topic/math-academy-id 1639 :topic/title "Complementary Angles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050754 :topic/math-academy-id 6207 :topic/title "Non-Adjacent Complementary Angles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050788 :topic/math-academy-id 6156 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations by Identifying Structure"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050926 :topic/math-academy-id 1045 :topic/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Fractional Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964 :topic/math-academy-id 1062 :topic/title "Expressing Rational Functions with Repeated Factors as Sums of Partial Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050974 :topic/math-academy-id 5137 :topic/title "Proving Consecutive Angle Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984 :topic/math-academy-id 410 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904 :topic/math-academy-id 812 :topic/title "Defining the Derivative Using Derivative Notation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049092 :topic/math-academy-id 2231 :topic/title "Selecting Units for Rates of Change"}] :topic/id #uuid "c490676a-a400-5088-9414-891ef037ec40" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064152 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064035 :question/id #uuid "b7051104-6e23-5ab2-8779-5d54a3c3f5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-270" :question/problem "Consider a spherical balloon with radius $r.$ As the balloon loses air, its volume $V$ decreases. What is the best interpretation of $V'(r)?$\n\n![](images/7f/7f52288ca4670375413ecd5283bc2d15fbb332af311ead07986c1526d25e338a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $V(r)$ gives the volume of a sphere with radius $r.$ So\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}V}{\\textrm{d}r} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }V}{\\textrm{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $V'(r)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes."} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :question/id #uuid "8378ead2-9247-55a0-acc8-2e6d0874ea09" :question/math-academy-id "e-271" :question/problem "The function $B(s)$ models a school's budget, in dollars, given the average number of students $s$ in each grade. What is the best interpretation of $B'(30)=2\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $B'(s)$ represents the rate at which the budget is changing at the moment when the school has an average of exactly $s$ students in each grade.\n\nTherefore, $B'(30) = 2\\,000$ means:\n\n*When the school has an average of $30$ students in each grade, its budget is increasing at a rate of $2\\,000$ dollars per student.*"} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064037 :question/id #uuid "750e7350-1434-56ef-80d8-7cf531108f96" :question/math-academy-id "e-273" :question/problem "On a particular day, the rate $E$ at which tourists enter a small country can be modeled as $E(t) = 50\\cdot 3^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, after midnight. The rate at which tourists leave the country can be modeled as $L(t)=100\\cdot 2^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is also the time in hours after midnight.\n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the number of tourists in the country at time $t$ is equal to the entry rate minus the exit rate at time $t,$ that is, \n\n$$\nE(t)-L(t).\n$$\n\nSince $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is ten hours after midnight, we have $t=10.$ Substituting $t=10,$ the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(10)-L(10) & =50\\cdot {3}^{0.2(10)}-100\\cdot {2}^{0.2(10)} \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is $50$ tourists per hour."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064114 :question/id #uuid "5831f44c-02c6-5a5b-a9e7-58c7ac36cb6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6112" :question/problem "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064115 :question/id #uuid "0f4baedc-a28a-5b45-97d7-d7e1196b21c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6113" :question/problem "Howard, the manager of a sci-fi convention, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of visitors to the convention, $t$ minutes after the convention has opened.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the convention\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the convention\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the convention\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the convention $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}N(t)}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }N}{\\textrm{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $N'(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the convention changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the convention at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = E(t) - L(t).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :topic/math-academy-id 296 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049236 :topic/math-academy-id 993 :topic/title "Estimating Derivatives Using a Backward Difference Quotient"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658 :topic/math-academy-id 620 :topic/title "Rates of Change in Applied Contexts"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049934 :topic/math-academy-id 992 :topic/title "Estimating Derivatives Using a Forward Difference Quotient"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901 :topic/math-academy-id 284 :topic/title "Calculating Velocity for Straight-Line Motion Using Differentiation"}] :topic/title "Interpreting the Meaning of the Derivative in Context"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404 :topic/math-academy-id 2204 :topic/title "Surds"}] :topic/id #uuid "ca183854-66cd-5b1f-9d29-03785c3216b8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044 :topic/math-academy-id 152 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304 :topic/math-academy-id 29 :topic/title "Adding and Subtracting Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163 :topic/math-academy-id 1167 :topic/title "Index Notation for Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}]}
